module("Accountability boundaries and ethical distress", "Produce an accountable action and review plan that addresses both individual and organisational pressures.", [
"An ethical decision is not complete when a worker chooses an option. The person needs an understandable explanation where appropriate, the agreed action needs an owner, and the service needs a way to review whether it happened. A useful note states the issue, relevant evidence, the person's expressed preference, the action taken, the reason and follow-up. Distinguish direct observation from another person's report. Correct errors through the authorised amendment process rather than silently replacing the original account. [S1, S2]",
"Boundaries protect the helping purpose of a relationship. A request for private contact, a gift or a favour may have cultural meaning as well as practical risk. Explore the context respectfully, check policy and discuss uncertainty in supervision. Do not promise secrecy, use personal accounts for case records or take on a task beyond competence simply to appear helpful. Explain the service route for further contact and complaints. In small communities, overlapping relationships may be unavoidable; they still require transparent management rather than concealment. [S1]",
"Workers may feel distress when they believe a justified action is blocked by staffing, scarce services, institutional instructions or competing demands. The qualitative review by Beadle and colleagues identifies individual, relational and organisational factors in moral distress and injury across health and social care. It does not establish a Nigerian prevalence rate or a guaranteed remedy. Use the evidence to ask about workload, decision authority and escalation routes as well as personal coping. Avoid diagnosing a colleague from a short description or suggesting that resilience alone can fix a service failure. [R1]",
"Build an action plan with two levels. At the practice level, request supervision, clarify responsibility and record unresolved risk within policy. At the service level, use approved channels to identify repeated access barriers, resource gaps or inappropriate instructions. Aggregate information carefully so that learning does not expose service users. Agree a review date and an accountable owner rather than making an open-ended complaint. If misconduct is suspected, use the relevant reporting procedure and evidence standards; do not make an allegation public on the basis of a classroom scenario or a journal discussion. [S1, R5]"],
"Fictional case: a team repeatedly cannot secure accessible referrals. The worker documents individual plans, seeks supervision on unresolved risks and raises an anonymised pattern through the service quality route. Personal support is useful, alongside review of the referral gap and workload.",
"Draft a two-level action plan for the team. Give one immediate action, one service improvement request, named role owners and a review date. Explain how the report avoids identifying service users.",
"A strong plan assigns actions within authority, preserves confidentiality and addresses the system barrier. It does not label a colleague with a diagnosis or guarantee that a suggested change will solve the problem.", ["S1","S2","R1","R5"], [
("Records distinguish observation, report and decision rationale.", "Which note best supports accountability?", "The person reported the barrier; the worker checked available options and recorded the agreed follow-up and reason.", ["The client was difficult.","Everything was resolved, with no supporting details.","The worker's assumption is recorded as a verified fact."], "A useful note identifies the evidence source, action and reasoning."),
("Boundary uncertainty needs transparent policy-based management.", "A client asks a worker to use a personal messaging account for case documents. What should the worker do?", "Explain the approved secure communication route and seek guidance if access remains difficult.", ["Accept because convenience overrides confidentiality.","Ask the client to send documents to the worker's relatives.","Store documents privately without telling the service."], "An authorised route protects information while allowing an access problem to be addressed."),
("Ethical distress may involve organisational constraints.", "Which response best reflects the moral-distress review?", "Examine workload and decision barriers alongside support for the worker.", ["Assume distress always shows personal weakness.","Treat self-care as a proven solution to every organisational failure.","Infer a clinical diagnosis from the review alone."], "The qualitative evidence describes multiple levels of influence and does not justify those conclusions."),
("Aggregate learning must protect confidentiality.", "How should a team report a recurring referral gap for service improvement?", "Use the approved route with appropriately anonymised patterns and an accountable action request.", ["Share identifiable case files in a public group.","Name clients in a social media post.","Avoid raising any pattern because no improvement is possible."], "Service learning and confidentiality can be addressed together through an authorised process."),
("Review plans need ownership and a date.", "Which follow-up plan is most actionable?", "The service lead will review the referral gap on an agreed date and report the next steps.", ["Someone should improve referrals eventually.","The learner guarantees that every future referral will succeed.","No review is required after an ethical decision."], "A role owner and review date allow unresolved concerns to be followed through without guaranteeing outcomes."),
]),
